        "original_id": "76c9a63a-e824-54ef-86ab-e21dac55f683",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Patrick Carpentier, Ken Schrader\n- Organizations: NASCAR Sprint Cup Series, NASCAR Craftsman Truck Series, Go FAS Racing\n- Products: Ford Fusion, Lincoln MKZ, Mercury Milan\n- Places: Ford Hermosillo Stamping & Assembly plant, Saugus Speedway\n- Events: 1997 CART season, 1995 NASCAR Craftsman Truck Series race\n- Concepts: CD3 platform, Rookie of the Year\n\nFacts:\n- In 1995, Saugus Speedway hosted one NASCAR Craftsman Truck Series event, and Ken Schrader won that race.\n- In 1997, Patrick Carpentier was the Rookie of the Year in the CART season.\n- Patrick Carpentier was born on August 13, 1971, and he is a Canadian professional auto racing driver.\n- Patrick Carpentier competed part-time in the NASCAR Sprint Cup Series, driving the No. 32 Ford Fusion for Go FAS Racing.\n- The Ford Fusion was introduced for the 2006 model year.\n- Ford manufactured the Ford Fusion at the Hermosillo Stamping and Assembly plant alongside the rebadged Lincoln MKZ.\n- Ford formerly manufactured the rebadged Mercury Milan with the Ford Fusion and Lincoln MKZ, and all three vehicles shared the CD3 platform.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "868d189e-6aa5-578d-9eed-cc98f660332e",
        "text": "Page: Patrick Carpentier. Sentence: Patrick Carpentier (born August 13, 1971) is a Canadian professional auto racing driver.\nPage: Patrick Carpentier. Sentence: He currently competes part-time in the NASCAR Sprint Cup Series, driving the No. 32 Ford Fusion for Go FAS Racing.\nPage: Ford Fusion (Americas). Sentence: Introduced for model year 2006, the Fusion is manufactured at Ford's Hermosillo Stamping & Assembly plant along with its rebadged variant the Lincoln MKZ — and formerly with its now discontinued rebadged variant, the Mercury Milan, all sharing the CD3 platform.\nPage: 1997 CART season. Sentence: Rookie of the Year was Patrick Carpentier.\nPage: Saugus Speedway. Sentence: The track hosted one NASCAR Craftsman Truck Series event in 1995, which was won by Ken Schrader.",
